       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Ibuprofen 1600 mg four times daily comes to 6,400 mg/day. That's twice the labeled maximum for adults of 3,200 mg/day. A single 1,600 mg dose is also double the usual maximum single dose of 800 mg. At this dose the risks of GI bleeding, kidney injury, and cardiovascular events go up a lot, and the extra dose adds no meaningful analgesic or anti-inflammatory benefit.\n\nHere are some options within labeled limits:\n- **Pain or fever:** 400\u2013600 mg PO every 6 hours as needed. Maximum 3,200 mg/day, though the lowest effective dose is usually preferred.\n- **Inflammatory conditions such as arthritis:** 800 mg PO three or four times daily. That is 2,400\u20133,200 mg/day.\n- **Maximum labeled dosing:** 800 mg PO four times daily (3,200 mg/day).\n\nPossible typos include 600 mg or 800 mg instead of 1600 mg. If you meant one of those, tell me the dose and frequency and I'll enter it. If you want something stronger than the maximum ibuprofen dose, you could add acetaminophen or consider another agent. That's your call or the prescriber's."
      }
